<UserControl x:Class="Smurftown.UI.MVVM.View.AccountCardView"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
             xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
             xmlns:md="https://github.com/fantasticfiasco/mvvm-dialogs"
             xmlns:viewModel="clr-namespace:Smurftown.UI.MVVM.ViewModel"
        xmlns:loc="clr-namespace:Smurftown.UI.MVVM"
             mc:Ignorable="d"
             d:DataContext="{d:DesignInstance viewModel:AccountCardViewModel}"
             d:DesignHeight="97" d:DesignWidth="1318"
             md:DialogServiceViews.IsRegistered="True">

    <!--
        ======================================================================
        An account ROW, not a card. Design "row" from the card catalog
        of 20.08.2026 (before that a 380x240 card, three per window row).
        ======================================================================

        md:DialogServiceViews.IsRegistered ABOVE - DO NOT REMOVE
        Without this attribute the edit button no longer works. MvvmDialogs
        looks for the owner window by searching among the REGISTERED views for one whose
        DataContext is the given ViewModel - here the AccountCardViewModel of this row.
        If the row is not registered, ShowDialog throws a ViewNotRegisteredException.
        Exactly that happened during the rebuild to the row layout: the file was rewritten,
        the attribute dropped out, and the error was invisible because the global
        exception handler turns it into a toast. This attribute is the only reason
        this file needs an md namespace at all.

        WHY A ROW
        Not denser, but comparable - equal values stand one above the other, and that's why
        the columns are FIXED and not content-dependent. The density advantage was eaten up
        by the enlargement of 21.08.2026: at 78 points height, 27 accounts need 3.2 screens,
        exactly as many as the cards before.

        NO SYMBOL, NO STRIP
        Until 20.08.2026 a tab column with a clickable symbol per game stood here;
        on 21.08.2026 first the last, no-longer-clickable symbol fell, and then the 3-point-
        wide accent strip. All said the same thing, and the game filter above says it anyway:
        it is exclusive, so every row shows the same game. What now carries the color is
        the TINT - the same accent as a gradient across the whole row width, plus the border
        under the pointer.

        WIDTH IS CALCULATED, NOT ESTIMATED
            1340 window (until 21.08.2026 1200 - the 140 went to the region filter of the
                 filter bar, which was already at around 1147 of 1160 points before)
            -  20 left margin - exactly that of the filter bar (DockPanel Margin 20,10,20,10)
            -  10 vertical scrollbar
            -  10 right margin: 20 MINUS the 10 of the scrollbar. It sits outside the
                  content, but the filter bar isn't inside the ScrollViewer at all - without this
                  deduction the row would stand 10 points further left than the bar above it.
            =1300 outer
            -   2 border
            =1298 inner, across three columns

            Column 0  Name             240  (12 margin + 228)
            Column 1  Panel            957  (14 margin + 943 content)
            Column 2  Buttons          101  (14 margin + 77 + 10 margin)
                                      ====
                                      1298

        The name column was 146 wide until 21.08.2026 and thereby cut off every battletag
        after about thirteen characters. 196 carried the longest possible one (16 bold
        characters at 13 plus hash and six digits at 11 is about 182 points), and 240 carry
        it together with the REGION ABBREVIATION behind it - that occupies 36 points with its
        spacing. The 44 are paid for out of the window's widening, not out of the panel's
        slack.

        HEIGHT
        91 plus 3 margin top and bottom = 97 per row. The number also stands above at
        d:DesignHeight. Two text lines of the name column fit inside, and so do the rank
        medal (78) and the separator ring of the hero strip (51).

        It was 78 until the medal grew 20 percent on 21.08.2026 (65 -> 78). The row
        HAD to grow with it: at 78 the medal would have filled the row from edge to edge.
        91 leaves it the same 6.5 points of slack top and bottom as before. The price shows
        in screens - 27 accounts now need around 3.8 instead of 3.2.

        THE VIEW FOLLOWS THE FILTER
        Which panel is shown is decided solely by GameFocus.Current - set by the exclusive
        game filter of the filter bar. Whoever filters on Overwatch above sees Overwatch in
        every row. Without a filter every row chooses HotS, otherwise its first game
        (AccountCardViewModel.PreferredGame).

        WPF TRAP THAT THE ROW DEPENDS ON
        Background, border and shadow are set in Border.Style and NOT as attributes on
        the Border. A locally set attribute has higher priority than any style trigger -
        writing Background="#1E1F24" directly makes the hover state ineffective, without
        anything reporting an error.
    -->

    <Border Margin="20,3,10,3" CornerRadius="8" BorderThickness="1" Height="91">
        <Border.Style>
            <Style TargetType="Border">
                <Setter Property="Background" Value="#1E1F24" />
                <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="#2B2D35" />
                <Style.Triggers>
                    <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                        <!-- Deliberately WEAK: the separator ring around the hero portraits is
                             painted in the base color and would visibly sit off if the hover
                             were strong. Two color steps are enough to recognize the row under
                             the pointer. -->
                        <Setter Property="Background" Value="#23252B" />
                        <!-- The border in the game's accent instead of neutral gray. It may be
                             strong because it only affects ONE row and only while
                             the pointer is on it - unlike the tint, which sits in every
                             one of the 27 rows at the same time. -->
                        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{Binding PanelHoverBorder}" />
                    </Trigger>
                </Style.Triggers>
            </Style>
        </Border.Style>

        <Grid>
            <!-- The tint: the same accent, fading from left to right, across the
                 whole row width. It lies UNDER everything else - a layer above it
                 would tint portraits, medal and numbers along with it.
                 Its own CornerRadius is mandatory and not cosmetic: a Border
                 doesn't clip its child to its own rounding, so this surface has to
                 carry it itself - 7 and not 8, because the outer frame
                 is 1 point thick.
                 Until 21.08.2026 a 3-point-wide accent strip on the left edge sat between
                 it and the content. It has been dropped: the tint says the same
                 thing across the whole width, and two carriers of the same statement are
                 one too many. Since then the content starts at 0 instead of at 3. -->
            <Border CornerRadius="7" Background="{Binding PanelTint}" />

            <Grid>
                <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="240" />
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

                <!-- ================================================ Column 0: Name ======
                     240 wide, of which 228 usable. The path there: 146/134 until
                     21.08.2026 (that's where it stopped at about thirteen characters), then
                     196/184 for the longest possible battletag - and now 240, because the
                     region abbreviation was added on the right. It occupies 36 points with
                     its spacing, leaving 192 for the battletag; of that, about 46 go to the
                     hash and the discriminator, so the name's 146.

                     The 44 points are paid for out of the window's widening to 1340.

                     Two lines. The read timestamp stands here and not in the panel: without
                     it none of the numbers next to it can be placed in context - 1,800 gold
                     from today and 1,800 gold from three months ago look the same. In the
                     panel it would have cost 130 points, in the name column it costs nothing. -->
                <StackPanel Grid.Column="0" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="12,0,0,0">
                    <!-- Top line: the account's name, followed by the region abbreviation. The
                         two name states sit SIDE BY SIDE in a horizontal panel, because
                         exactly one is always visible - the collapsed one occupies no width,
                         and the abbreviation moves to the same spot in both cases. -->
                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal">
                        <!-- The battletag is read and not typed, so a fresh account
                             doesn't have one - then the email carries the row. An empty name
                             plus "#" would look like a half-loaded value. -->
                        <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Visibility="{Binding BattletagVisibility}">
                            <TextBlock Text="{Binding Account.Name, UpdateSourceTrigger=PropertyChanged}"
                                       Foreground="White" FontWeight="Bold" FontSize="13"
                                       VerticalAlignment="Center" MaxWidth="146"
                                       TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                                       ToolTip="{Binding Account.Name, UpdateSourceTrigger=PropertyChanged}" />
                            <TextBlock Text="#" Foreground="#8F93A0" FontWeight="Light" FontSize="11"
                                       VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="3,0,0,0" />
                            <TextBlock Text="{Binding Account.Discriminator}" Foreground="#8F93A0"
                                       FontWeight="Light" FontSize="11" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                        </StackPanel>
                        <TextBlock Text="{Binding NameFallback}" Visibility="{Binding NameFallbackVisibility}"
                                   Foreground="#C6C8D0" FontWeight="DemiBold" FontSize="11.5"
                                   VerticalAlignment="Center" MaxWidth="192"
                                   TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                                   ToolTip="{loc:Str row.noBattletagHint}" />

                        <!-- THE REGION ABBREVIATION NAMES THE ROW. Since 21.08.2026 an account
                             appears once per chosen region in the list, and the two
                             rows look identical without this badge - except for rank,
                             heroes and gold, i.e. exactly the values one wants to compare.

                             It is ALWAYS there, even with only one region: a value that
                             sometimes appears and sometimes doesn't leaves it open, when
                             skimming, whether it is missing or doesn't apply. -->
                        <Border Background="#2A2D36" BorderBrush="#3A3D46" BorderThickness="1"
                                CornerRadius="3" Padding="5,1" Margin="6,0,0,0"
                                VerticalAlignment="Center" ToolTip="{Binding RegionHint}">
                            <TextBlock Text="{Binding RegionLabel}" Foreground="#9BA0AE"
                                       FontSize="9.5" FontWeight="DemiBold" />
                        </Border>
                    </StackPanel>
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding ReadAtText}" Foreground="#63677A" FontSize="9.5"
                               Margin="0,2,0,0" TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" />
                </StackPanel>

                <!-- =============================================== Column 1: Panel ======
                     Two states stacked on top of each other, always exactly one visible.
                     Laid out horizontally: the same values as on the earlier card, but in a
                     row instead of in a block.
                     The currencies are docked at fixed width on the RIGHT, the rest fills
                     the left. Budget of the 847:

                         right    currencies 4x59                         236
                         left     medal 59+10                              69
                                  hero strip 11x47, overlap 13            387
                                  "+n"                                      28
                                  counter                                  56
                                                                          ====
                                  540 of 611 - 71 slack

                     THE PENALTY TRIANGLE NO LONGER STANDS HERE. On 21.08.2026 it became a
                     red number in the row's upper-left corner and freed up 47 points; together
                     with the 167 from the button column and the 3 from the accent
                     strip, that's 217. Of that, 102 go into three more
                     hero circles (eight became eleven), the remaining 115 stay SLACK.

                     THE SLACK IS DELIBERATE and not a leftover nobody has distributed yet.
                     Thirteen circles would fit (455); but a row that stands full from left to
                     right reads harder than one with a pause. Whoever
                     adds something here has the room - it then costs no hero circle.

                     IT SITS ON BOTH SIDES, not only on the right: the medal is docked on the
                     left (81 with spacing), the currencies on the right (236), and the strip
                     sits centered in the 626 points between them. 626 - 471 (strip 387,
                     "+n" 28, counter 56) is 155, so about 77 per side.

                     The slack jumped twice on 21.08.2026: from 121 to 71, when the
                     name column grew from 146 to 196 - and to 155, when the window for the
                     region filter went from 1200 to 1340 and 96 of the 140 points arrived here.

                     THE ROW HEIGHT IS THE SECOND LIMIT. The medal is 78 tall in a
                     91-tall row, the separator ring 51 - for both, 6.5 and 20 points of slack
                     remain top and bottom respectively. Taller is only possible via the row,
                     and that costs screens: 28 accounts need around 3.8 at 91 instead of the
                     2.5 they needed at 60 - and with a second region it becomes twice as
                     many rows.

                     NO more rank text. Next to the medal, until 20.08.2026, stood a
                     short version ("Gold 3", "Placements pending", "Unranked") with a fixed
                     width of 112 points. It said little that the image next to it doesn't
                     already show: the 28 medals carry tier AND division, and with open
                     placement matches the medal is dimmed. The plain text still stands
                     in the tooltip (RankName) and covers all three cases there.
                     The 112 points now sit in the hero strip: 12 circles instead of 8. -->
                <Grid Grid.Column="1" Margin="14,0,0,0">

                    <!-- ===================================================== HotS ======= -->
                    <DockPanel Visibility="{Binding HotsPanelVisibility}" LastChildFill="True">

                        <!-- Gold, shards, gems, chests - right-aligned, so they stand one
                             above the other across all rows. That is the reason
                             the list was built in the first place.
                             The four symbols are XAML shapes and not PNGs: a coin, a
                             triangle, a hexagon and a chest at 13 points are geometry
                             and not image material. Colors measured from the game header bar.
                             A dash instead of a number means "never read yet" -
                             not "has nothing"; a 0 would be a statement that doesn't
                             exist without reading. -->
                        <Grid DockPanel.Dock="Right" Width="236" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                              ToolTip="{Binding CurrencyHint}">
                            <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                                <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
                                <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
                                <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
                                <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
                            </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

                            <StackPanel Grid.Column="0" Orientation="Horizontal" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                                <Ellipse Width="13" Height="13" Fill="#E3C34A" Stroke="#8A6E1E"
                                         StrokeThickness="1.5" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                                <TextBlock Text="{Binding GoldText}" Foreground="#C9CDD8" FontSize="11.5"
                                           Margin="5,0,0,0" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                            </StackPanel>

                            <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" Orientation="Horizontal" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                                <Path Data="M0,0 L13,0 L6.5,11 Z" Fill="#DCE2F0" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                                <TextBlock Text="{Binding ShardsText}" Foreground="#C9CDD8" FontSize="11.5"
                                           Margin="5,0,0,0" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                            </StackPanel>

                            <StackPanel Grid.Column="2" Orientation="Horizontal" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                                <Path Data="M6.5,0 L12,3.2 L12,9.6 L6.5,12.8 L1,9.6 L1,3.2 Z"
                                      Fill="#3FCBBB" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                                <TextBlock Text="{Binding GemsText}" Foreground="#C9CDD8" FontSize="11.5"
                                           Margin="5,0,0,0" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                            </StackPanel>

                            <!-- Chest: arched lid, dark band underneath, box, lock.
                                 Four paths - with three, at 13 points an orange blob was
                                 visible and no chest. The band separates lid from box and is
                                 the part that makes the shape legible. -->
                            <StackPanel Grid.Column="3" Orientation="Horizontal" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                                <Grid Width="14" Height="13" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                                    <Path Data="M0.8,5.4 C0.8,1.9 3.5,0.4 7,0.4 C10.5,0.4 13.2,1.9 13.2,5.4 Z"
                                          Fill="#D8A65E" />
                                    <Path Data="M0.8,5.4 L13.2,5.4 L13.2,7.1 L0.8,7.1 Z" Fill="#6E4B22" />
                                    <Path Data="M0.8,7.1 L13.2,7.1 L13.2,12.4 L0.8,12.4 Z" Fill="#B9884C" />
                                    <Path Data="M5.9,4.2 L8.1,4.2 L8.1,8.6 L5.9,8.6 Z" Fill="#F0D28A" />
                                </Grid>
                                <TextBlock Text="{Binding ChestsText}" Foreground="#C9CDD8" FontSize="11.5"
                                           Margin="5,0,0,0" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                            </StackPanel>
                        </Grid>

                        <!-- The rank medal docks on the LEFT, the currencies on the right, and
                             the hero strip sits centered between them.

                             WHY DOCKED AND NOT IN ONE ROW: in a horizontal row
                             everything sticks to the left edge, and the 121 points of slack then
                             sat entirely between counter and currencies - a hole at exactly
                             one spot. Now they split across both sides of the strip,
                             around 60 per side.

                             THE PRICE: the first hero circle no longer sits at the same spot
                             in every row. An account with three heroes has a shorter
                             strip, and a centered short strip starts further to the right.
                             The medal and the currencies still align with each other -
                             and they carry the numbers that matter for comparing.
                             The strip is a sample either way, not the statement.

                             The game symbol stood here until 21.08.2026 and is gone without
                             replacement: the game filter chooses the game for ALL rows, so it
                             showed the same thing in every row.

                             With pending placement matches the medal is dimmed
                             (RankOpacity) - the same value as in the edit dialog. No real
                             grayscale: WPF's FormatConvertedBitmap with Gray8 has no
                             alpha channel, the cut-out medal would get a black
                             blob. -->
                        <Image DockPanel.Dock="Left"
                               Source="{Binding RankImageSource}"
                               Opacity="{Binding RankOpacity}"
                               Visibility="{Binding RankVisibility}"
                               ToolTip="{Binding RankName}"
                               RenderOptions.BitmapScalingMode="HighQuality"
                               VerticalAlignment="Center" Height="78" Width="71"
                               Margin="0,0,10,0" />

                        <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                                    VerticalAlignment="Center">

                            <!-- Hero strip. ELEVEN circles at 47 points with 13 points of
                                 overlap occupy 387 points. The path there: six at 26
                                 (121), eight at 30 (184) with the room from the dropped
                                 tab column, twelve at 30 (272) with that of the rank text,
                                 eleven at 36 (296), on 21.08.2026 bigger instead of more -
                                 eight at 47, because medal and circles grew by 30 percent -
                                 and back to eleven the same day, when penalty triangle, three
                                 buttons and the accent strip freed up 217 points.
                                 FOURTEEN WOULD FIT (489 of 508). Eleven were chosen, because
                                 the row would otherwise stand full from left to right; the 121
                                 points before it are slack on purpose.
                                 The overlap grew along with the circle size (10 to
                                 13), so the circles sit as tightly as before.
                                 The strip remains a sample; the statement stands as a
                                 number next to it.
                                 The margin on the ItemsControl compensates for the overlap of
                                 the FIRST portrait - it is always the amount of the
                                 overlap, otherwise the strip protrudes to the left. -->
                            <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding HeroChips}"
                                          Visibility="{Binding HeroChipsVisibility}"
                                          VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="13,0,0,0"
                                          ToolTip="{Binding HeroStripHint}">
                                <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                                    <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                                        <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" />
                                    </ItemsPanelTemplate>
                                </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                                <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                                    <DataTemplate>
                                        <Grid Width="47" Height="47" Margin="-13,0,0,0"
                                              ToolTip="{Binding Tooltip}">
                                            <Ellipse>
                                                <Ellipse.Fill>
                                                    <ImageBrush ImageSource="{Binding ImageSource}"
                                                                Stretch="UniformToFill" />
                                                </Ellipse.Fill>
                                            </Ellipse>
                                            <Ellipse Stroke="{Binding RoleBrush}" StrokeThickness="2" />
                                            <!-- Separator ring, so the overlapping circles
                                                 stand out from one another. It is a HOLE and
                                                 therefore carries the color that lies behind
                                                 it at its spot - since the tint, that is no
                                                 longer the row's base color, but a value that
                                                 GameVisuals derives from the same gradient. Set
                                                 by hand it would drift away from it at the
                                                 next tweak.
                                                 The hover stays weak nonetheless: it shifts
                                                 the ground, not the ring. -->
                                            <Ellipse Stroke="{Binding DataContext.StripSeparator, RelativeSource={RelativeSource AncestorType=ItemsControl}}"
                                                     StrokeThickness="2" Width="51" Height="51" />
                                        </Grid>
                                    </DataTemplate>
                                </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                            </ItemsControl>

                            <TextBlock Text="{Binding HeroOverflow}"
                                       Visibility="{Binding HeroOverflowVisibility}"
                                       Foreground="#7E93AE" FontSize="11" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                       Margin="8,0,0,0" />

                            <!-- Without heroes a sentence stands there, not an empty strip: that
                                 would be a hole in the layout and would leave it open whether
                                 it was never read or there is nothing there. -->
                            <TextBlock Text="{loc:Str row.noHeroes}"
                                       Visibility="{Binding HeroEmptyVisibility}"
                                       Foreground="#5A5E6C" FontSize="10.5" FontStyle="Italic"
                                       VerticalAlignment="Center" />

                            <!-- The number is the statement, the strip before it only the sample.
                                 "– / 90" means never read; "0 / 90" would claim the account
                                 owned not a single hero. -->
                            <TextBlock Text="{Binding HeroCountText}" FontSize="10.5"
                                       Foreground="#7E93AE" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                       Margin="10,0,0,0" ToolTip="{Binding HeroStripHint}" />
                        </StackPanel>
                    </DockPanel>

                    <!-- ================================================ without data ======
                         For Overwatch, WoW and Diablo. For these three there is only
                         one bool each in BattlenetAccount and nothing else; the box says exactly
                         that and names what will stand there one day. An empty box would
                         look like an error, a named one is a deliberate choice.
                         Dashed as a Rectangle and not as a Border: Border cannot do
                         dashing. Same shape as the empty hero filter in the
                         filter bar. -->
                    <Grid Visibility="{Binding NoDataVisibility}" Margin="0,9,10,9">
                        <Rectangle Stroke="#3A3D46" StrokeThickness="1.5" StrokeDashArray="3 2"
                                   RadiusX="5" RadiusY="5" />
                        <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                    HorizontalAlignment="Left" Margin="14,0,14,0">
                            <TextBlock Text="{Binding NoDataTitle}" Foreground="#8F93A0"
                                       FontSize="11.5" FontWeight="SemiBold"
                                       VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                            <TextBlock Text="{Binding NoDataHint}" Foreground="#5A5E6C"
                                       FontSize="10.5" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                       Margin="10,0,0,0" TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" />
                        </StackPanel>
                    </Grid>
                </Grid>

                <!-- ============================================= Column 2: Buttons ======
                     TWO round buttons, both open a menu: play - and everything
                     else. Budget: 36 + 5 + 36 = 77.

                     Until 21.08.2026 a rectangular "Start" button (96) and
                     FOUR 32-size buttons for email, password, edit and archive stood here,
                     making 244 points. The four moved into the three-dot menu: three of
                     them are rarely needed, and side by side they looked like a
                     toolbar on a row that doesn't have one.

                     ROUND like the plus button of the filter bar. The start button has since
                     carried a triangle instead of the word "Start" - with two round buttons
                     side by side, one reads the pair and not the label.

                     The column is Auto width and sits on the right: the 167 points gained
                     don't move the buttons, but make the panel wider on the left. There
                     they end up in the hero strip.

                     STAYSOPEN=FALSE only closes on a click OUTSIDE. A chosen
                     entry has to close itself - each of the five commands resets its
                     menu toggle as its first instruction (RunStartOption, CopyUsername,
                     CopyPassword, OpenSettings, ToggleArchive). Whoever adds an entry
                     that doesn't go through one of these has to account for that.
                     The 10-point margin on the menu surface is the room for the shadow - the
                     offsets pull it back out again, so the menu sticks to the button. -->
                <StackPanel Grid.Column="2" Orientation="Horizontal" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                            Margin="14,0,10,0">
                    <ToggleButton x:Name="StartMenuButton"
                                  Style="{StaticResource BattlenetRoundMenuButtonTheme}"
                                  Background="#1A73E8"
                                  Visibility="{Binding StartVisibility}"
                                  IsChecked="{Binding StartMenuOpen, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                  ToolTip="{loc:Str row.startMenuHint}">
                        <!-- Offset two points to the right: a triangle otherwise looks shifted
                             to the left, because its area sits to the left of its center point. -->
                        <Path Data="M0,0 L12,7 L0,14 Z" Fill="White" Margin="2,0,0,0" />
                    </ToggleButton>

                    <!-- RIGHT-ALIGNED UNDER ITS BUTTON, exactly like the actions menu below:
                         36 (button) - 360 (surface) - 10 (shadow margin) = -334.

                         Until 21.08.2026, -10 and MinWidth 270 stood here, and the menu opened
                         to the right - at 1340 window width it hung about 170 points
                         outside the window on the desktop. It was noticed only when it
                         was cut off in a screenshot for the README; a popup is its
                         own window, so WPF doesn't push it back on its own as long as
                         there is still screen next to it.

                         Width and not MinWidth, for the same reason as below: with a
                         surface that aligns to the longest entry, the offset would be
                         guesswork.

                         360 AND NOT 300, since the interface has been translated. For the
                         subtitle, after icon (22), spacing (10) and margins (32), exactly
                         surface minus 64 remains - at 300 that's 236 points. Measured with
                         FormattedText, Segoe UI 11, in all four languages:

                             German        291.6   "starten, anmelden, alle Truhen oeffnen, ..."
                             French        279.9
                             Spanish       256.5
                             English       224.1

                         English fit, the other three didn't - and the truncation is
                         silent: the text simply stood shorter there. It was noticed only
                         in a screenshot of the running application, not when building and not
                         in the log. 360 carries the longest with 4 points of reserve.

                         The right edge stays put where it was - the formula above
                         keeps it flush with the button edge; the menu grows wider to the
                         LEFT, and there is room there. The actions menu below stays at 240: its
                         longest entry ("Copier le mot de passe") measures 134.7 of 176. -->
                    <Popup PlacementTarget="{Binding ElementName=StartMenuButton}"
                           Placement="Bottom" VerticalOffset="-6" HorizontalOffset="-334"
                           StaysOpen="False" AllowsTransparency="True" PopupAnimation="Fade"
                           IsOpen="{Binding StartMenuOpen, Mode=TwoWay}">
                        <Border Style="{StaticResource BattlenetMenuSurfaceTheme}" Width="360">
                            <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding StartOptions}">
                                <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                                    <DataTemplate>
                                        <Button Style="{StaticResource BattlenetMenuItemTheme}"
                                                IsEnabled="{Binding Enabled}"
                                                Command="{Binding Command}"
                                                CommandParameter="{Binding Path=Mode}">
                                            <Grid>
                                                <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                                                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                                                    <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
                                                </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                                                <Image Grid.Column="0" Source="{Binding Icon}"
                                                       Width="22" Height="22" Margin="0,0,10,0"
                                                       VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                                       RenderOptions.BitmapScalingMode="HighQuality" />
                                                <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                                                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding Label}" Foreground="White"
                                                               FontWeight="DemiBold" FontSize="13" />
                                                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding Hint}" Foreground="#8F93A0"
                                                               FontSize="11" Margin="0,1,0,0" />
                                                </StackPanel>
                                            </Grid>
                                        </Button>
                                    </DataTemplate>
                                </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                            </ItemsControl>
                        </Border>
                    </Popup>

                    <ToggleButton x:Name="ActionsMenuButton" Margin="5,0,0,0"
                                  Style="{StaticResource BattlenetRoundMenuButtonTheme}"
                                  IsChecked="{Binding ActionsMenuOpen, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                  ToolTip="{loc:Str row.actionsMenuHint}">
                        <!-- Three dots as ellipses and not as an ellipsis character: its
                             spacing depends on the font, three circles don't. -->
                        <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal">
                            <Ellipse Width="3.5" Height="3.5" Fill="#E4E6EC" Margin="1.75,0" />
                            <Ellipse Width="3.5" Height="3.5" Fill="#E4E6EC" Margin="1.75,0" />
                            <Ellipse Width="3.5" Height="3.5" Fill="#E4E6EC" Margin="1.75,0" />
                        </StackPanel>
                    </ToggleButton>

                    <!-- Right-aligned under the button, and the offset is calculated:
                         36 (button) - 240 (surface) - 10 (shadow margin) = -214. The column
                         sits at the right window edge, a menu opening to the right
                         would run off - WPF would then push it back onto the screen, but
                         to a spot nobody chose.
                         That's why Width and not MinWidth stands here: with a surface that
                         aligns to the longest entry, the offset would be guesswork. -->
                    <Popup PlacementTarget="{Binding ElementName=ActionsMenuButton}"
                           Placement="Bottom" VerticalOffset="-6" HorizontalOffset="-214"
                           StaysOpen="False" AllowsTransparency="True" PopupAnimation="Fade"
                           IsOpen="{Binding ActionsMenuOpen, Mode=TwoWay}">
                        <Border Style="{StaticResource BattlenetMenuSurfaceTheme}" Width="240">
                            <StackPanel>
                                <Button Style="{StaticResource BattlenetMenuItemTheme}"
                                        Command="{Binding CopyUsernameCommand}">
                                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal">
                                        <Image Source="pack://application:,,,/UI/Images/user.png"
                                               Width="17" Height="17" Margin="2,0,12,0"
                                               VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                               RenderOptions.BitmapScalingMode="HighQuality" />
                                        <TextBlock Text="{loc:Str row.copyEmail}" Foreground="White"
                                                   FontWeight="DemiBold" FontSize="13"
                                                   VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                                    </StackPanel>
                                </Button>

                                <Button Style="{StaticResource BattlenetMenuItemTheme}"
                                        Command="{Binding CopyPasswordCommand}">
                                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal">
                                        <Image Source="pack://application:,,,/UI/Images/lock.png"
                                               Width="17" Height="17" Margin="2,0,12,0"
                                               VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                               RenderOptions.BitmapScalingMode="HighQuality" />
                                        <TextBlock Text="{loc:Str row.copyPassword}" Foreground="White"
                                                   FontWeight="DemiBold" FontSize="13"
                                                   VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                                    </StackPanel>
                                </Button>

                                <Button Style="{StaticResource BattlenetMenuItemTheme}"
                                        Command="{Binding OpenSettingsCommand}">
                                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal">
                                        <Image Source="pack://application:,,,/UI/Images/settings.png"
                                               Width="17" Height="17" Margin="2,0,12,0"
                                               VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                               RenderOptions.BitmapScalingMode="HighQuality" />
                                        <TextBlock Text="{loc:Str row.editAccount}" Foreground="White"
                                                   FontWeight="DemiBold" FontSize="13"
                                                   VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                                    </StackPanel>
                                </Button>

                                <!-- Archive, NOT delete - the reasoning stands in the
                                     tooltip, because it's too long for a label.
                                     The box is drawn from paths and not a PNG, exactly
                                     like the four currency symbols. The arrow turns with
                                     the direction of the gesture: inward when archiving, outward
                                     when restoring.
                                     The cutout in the arrow carries the color of the MENU
                                     SURFACE (#1E2027) and not that of the row - the box stands
                                     here now. -->
                                <Button Style="{StaticResource BattlenetMenuItemTheme}"
                                        Command="{Binding ArchiveCommand}"
                                        ToolTip="{Binding ArchiveHint}">
                                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal">
                                        <Grid Width="17" Height="16" Margin="2,0,12,0"
                                              VerticalAlignment="Center">
                                            <Path Data="M0,0 L17,0 L17,4.1 L0,4.1 Z" Fill="#C9CBD2" />
                                            <Path Data="M1.8,5.7 L15.2,5.7 L15.2,16 L1.8,16 Z"
                                                  Fill="#8F93A0" />
                                            <Path Data="M8.5,13.7 L5.2,9.6 L7.3,9.6 L7.3,6.8 L9.7,6.8 L9.7,9.6 L11.8,9.6 Z"
                                                  Fill="#1E2027" Visibility="{Binding ArchiveDownVisibility}" />
                                            <Path Data="M8.5,6.8 L11.8,10.9 L9.7,10.9 L9.7,13.7 L7.3,13.7 L7.3,10.9 L5.2,10.9 Z"
                                                  Fill="#1E2027" Visibility="{Binding ArchiveUpVisibility}" />
                                        </Grid>
                                        <TextBlock Text="{Binding ArchiveLabel}" Foreground="White"
                                                   FontWeight="DemiBold" FontSize="13"
                                                   VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                                    </StackPanel>
                                </Button>
                            </StackPanel>
                        </Border>
                    </Popup>
                </StackPanel>
            </Grid>

            <!-- PENALTY GAMES. Only the number, outlined in red, in the upper-left corner - and
                 only when > 0. Until 21.08.2026 it stood as a warning triangle plus badge in the
                 HotS panel and cost 47 points there; the warning that sat in the triangle
                 is carried just the same by the red ring, and the count stands in it anyway.

                 IT SITS OUTSIDE THE PANEL and thus outside the column calculation: the
                 corner is free because the name column sits vertically centered. A change of
                 the game filter therefore leaves it standing - it belongs to the account, not to
                 the HotS panel. For an account without the HotS checkbox the value is 0, so invisible.

                 IT IS THE LAST CHILD of the outer Grid, and that is not an order chosen
                 out of convenience: in WPF the later child draws on top. Standing further
                 up it would sit under the content - visible only as long as nothing draws over
                 it, and its tooltip would be a matter of luck.

                 ONLY THE TRIANGLE, NO NUMBER. How many penalty games are open is told by the
                 tooltip - that question comes up less often than whether any are open at all.

                 18 POINTS AND INSIDE THE ROW. An intermediate state from 21.08.2026 had it
                 at 28 points, half protruding out of the corner; that was noticeable, but it
                 protruded over the preceding row and forced AccountsView.xaml into a 14-point
                 margin, because the list's ScrollViewer would otherwise have clipped exactly
                 the topmost row. Both are gone again.

                 Margin 6,5: with CornerRadius 8 the arc runs from (0,8) to (8,0), at x=6
                 the edge already sits at y=0.6 - the symbol stays clear. Downward it reaches
                 to y=23; the name column sits vertically CENTERED and its text starts only
                 at around y=28, so it collides with nothing. 18 is thus also the upper limit
                 for the corner - whoever wants more catches the overhang plus margin again.

                 IT COSTS NO WIDTH. The image is a child of the outer Grid and thus sits
                 OVER the three columns, not in one of them - the row's column budget is
                 unaffected by it, and a change of the game filter leaves it standing. -->
            <Image Source="pack://application:,,,/UI/Images/penalty.png"
                   Width="18" Height="18"
                   RenderOptions.BitmapScalingMode="HighQuality"
                   HorizontalAlignment="Left" VerticalAlignment="Top" Margin="6,5,0,0"
                   Visibility="{Binding PenaltyVisibility}"
                   ToolTip="{Binding PenaltyName}" />
        </Grid>
    </Border>
</UserControl>
